criteria.descriptionMarkdown:
Changing the mode at runtime:

- takes effect on the next frame, and that frame equals the from-scratch frame;
- releases retained records, kept layout nodes and layer tiles that the disabled mechanism owned, with no growth after 1,000 on/off toggles under churn;
- with every switch off, makes no `fast::` work beyond reading the switch. Off frames record zero fast-mode counters.
- One exception is not a fast-mode mechanism: the hover dependency of gpux's cached views (`AnyView::cached`). The sprint-1 oracle found that cached views replayed stale hover styles, so cached views record the hovers they read (`fast/dependencies.rs`) and are replayed only while each is unchanged, and a frame that replayed one whose hovers this frame's hitboxes changed is drawn again before it is shown. This is gpux's cached-view behaviour in every mode, fast mode off included; it records no mechanism counter.

Verified by the `fast::tests::switches` group.

`FastStats` exists only under `test-support`, `bench-support` or `diagnostics`. It is read per frame and accumulated across a run. It counts:

- views built, built at retained layout, reused, spliced and prebuilt;
- Taffy nodes claimed, style writes, children writes, nodes released and lingering;
- measured leaves carried, replayed and re-measured;
- lines shaped;
- primitives painted and copied;
- draw orders replayed and computed;
- scroll layers composited, repainted and bypassed (each bypass with its reason), tiles rasterized and composited, and list rows rendered and kept.

Production code never references it: a default-feature `cargo check -p gpux` fails if ungated code does. Verified by the `fast::tests::stats` group and that build.

A new `#[gpux::bench]` suite, `fast_mode` in `gpux-platform` (`[[bench]]` with `bench-support`), runs every scenario in every cumulative mode: `off`, `layout`, `views`, `splice`, `layers`. Ids have the form `fast/<scenario>/<mode>`.

Scenarios are shared fixtures in `gpux` under `bench-support`/`test-support`:
- `still-frame`: 60 panel views of 64 labels, nothing changed;
- `one-panel`, `six-panels`, `all-panels`: panels notified per frame;
- `table-stream`: a data table with ticking cells of changing and equal width;
- `settings-toggle` and `tree-expand`: layout changes;
- `form-typing`: a focused editable text;
- `hover-sweep`: the pointer moving across rows;
- `scroll-child-view`, `scroll-same-view`, `scroll-uniform-list`, `scroll-list` and `chat-scroll`: real wheel events;
- `sticky-scroll`: sticky headers in a scroll area;
- `effect-scroll`: an `effect_frame` backdrop blur inside a scroll area, a negative control;
- `animated-frame`: a negative control.

Each measured loop asserts its scenario's work counters, for example that the changed cell re-rendered and the still frame rendered nothing in `views`. A faster frame that skipped required work fails.

`cargo bench -p gpux-platform --features bench-support --bench fast_mode -- --test` runs every id once and passes.

The deterministic counter-attribution test group `crates/gpui/tests/fast_mode_attribution.rs` runs every scenario for a fixed number of frames in every mode and asserts the counter shape that attributes the benefit. For example:

- `still-frame`: `views` reuses all 60 panels and renders none;
- `layout`: writes no Taffy node;
- `table-stream`: equal-width ticks replay and leave the row clean;
- `one-panel`: `splice` rebuilds exactly one view;
- `scroll-child-view`: `layers` composites on every scrolled frame after promotion and renders no view.

Expectations are exact counts or exact bounds, and are machine-independent.

A test-support frame description (`fast::verify::describe_frame`) captures:

- every scene primitive family, gpux's included: quads, shadows, underlines, monochrome, subpixel and polychrome sprites, paths, surfaces, visual effects, HTML islands and elements, screen-effect regions and requests;
- for each primitive, its draw order, bounds, content mask with radii, transform, opacity and colour;
- every hitbox: bounds, mask, behaviour and the point-local hit-test order;
- cursor styles, tooltip requests, tab stops, the mouse-listener count and the event type each listener is registered for (a listener's dispatch phase is an argument it is called with, not part of its registration, so the description counts listeners per event type), dispatch tree shape and focus path, accessed element-state keys, DevKit node tree, and input-handler presence.

It reports the first difference, with the seed and change history. Verified by the `fast::tests::oracle` group, which checks that a deliberately mutated field of each kind is detected.

The randomized oracle drives two windows with the same view through the same history. The reference window forgets all retained state and refreshes before every frame. The frames must match after every step.

The history generator covers at least these change kinds:
- notify, update with notify, update without notify, and a notify while drawing;
- global write, `has_global` presence change, unobserved model read;
- `ScrollHandle` and `ListState` scroll, and programmatic scroll;
- hover, press, focus and key;
- editable-text edit;
- keyed child insert, remove and reorder;
- window resize and scale change;
- an animation frame;
- fixed and sticky scrolling, a top-layer overlay, `deferred` and `Deferred::unclipped`;
- `effect_frame`, `animated_frame` and `transition_frame`;
- a drag.

`SEED` and `ITERATIONS` control seeds, and the default run is at least 16 seeds of 60 steps. A non-vacuity assertion requires that the incremental window really reused work: cached views in this sprint, and each later mode in its own oracle group. Verified by the `fast::tests::oracle` group.

gpux-owned hot paths stop calling `Window::refresh()`, which disables every cache for the frame, where a scoped invalidation is enough. These paths now notify the owning view, plus registered scroll dependents where they apply:

- programmatic scroll and `ScrollActivity` animation steps (`div.rs`, `list.rs`, `uniform_list.rs`);
- hover, active and group-hover style changes, and clearing a press released elsewhere (`div.rs`);
- editable-text and text-selection redraws;
- key-dispatch redraws.

`refresh()` stays only for whole-window changes: resize, scale, appearance, font installation, GPU recovery, root replacement, inspector toggles and the inspected element, and the changes any view may read or that draw window-level chrome over every view: focus moves, the input modality, window activation and hover status, the visual viewport and insets, accessibility activation, the active drag preview and tooltips. Each kept site has a comment saying why. Paths that refreshed while drawing, where `refresh()` does nothing, lose the call.

Verified by the `fast::tests::scoped_invalidation` group. It asserts that each converted path redraws correctly against the oracle and leaves `refreshing` false, a press released outside its element included.

With layout retention on, an element finds its Taffy node by a 64-bit key:

- a hash of the path from the root, each step being the element's `ElementId` or its position among unidentified siblings;
- for `uniform_list` and `list` items without an id, the item index;
- for layout requested during prepaint (`layout_as_root`, `prepaint_as_root`), a prepaint-scope salt.

Inserting, removing or reordering keyed siblings leaves every other node unwritten, so Taffy's cache keeps it clean, as shown by the counters. A duplicate key within a frame gets a fresh transient node. With layout retention off, today's ordinal pool behaviour is unchanged.

Verified by the `fast::tests::layout_keys` group.

Layout retention composes with gpux layout features:

- sticky candidate discovery and fixed containing blocks;
- absolute edge snapping;
- the request-to-prepaint style handoff and conditional prepaint style recompute;
- `container_query`, `uniform_list` and variable-height `list`;
- editable text, and gpux's taffy feature set (no `content_size`).

The randomized oracle in layout-only mode (`FastMode { layout_retention: true, .. off }`) matches the from-scratch frame over every change kind, with a non-vacuity assertion that nodes were kept clean. Verified by the `fast::tests::oracle_layout` group.

These reads are recorded:

- entity reads;
- global reads, and global presence through `has_global` (presence only);
- `ScrollHandle` and `ListState` reads, through a version that changes only when state really changes. Idempotent `scroll_to`, `set_offset` and `scroll_to_end` do not bump it, and gpux's thumb publication and dependent views keep their single invalidation;
- ambient window input: `mouse_position`, `modifiers`, `capslock` and gpux's `pressed_mouse_button`;
- hover reads through `HitboxId::is_hovered`.

These changes are stamped:
- entity update, notify, insert and release;
- global write, insert and remove;
- ambient input changes after event dispatch, and when the window reloads them from the platform (at the start of a draw, on activation);
- writes while drawing.

Notify-versus-update rules follow gpui-fast:
- a pure notify does not invalidate readers;
- an update without notify counts only inside a notified view;
- a write while drawing counts as a change for foreign readers.

`ElementInputHandler` platform queries are exempt. Changes are stamped from the first recording on: an app that never records (view retention off in every window) pays only a branch per hook. Verified by the `fast::tests::dependencies` group, with one test per read kind and per rule.

Recording nests:

- a record's own dependencies exclude those of nested records;
- its subtree dependencies include them;
- replaying a reused record re-registers its accessed entities, so window invalidation routing (`dirty_views`, `mark_view_dirty`) still reaches the window.

Verified by the `fast::tests::dependency_nesting` group.

Each dependency kind invalidates exactly the views that read it, and no others:

- entity update with notify;
- a write while drawing;
- global write and presence;
- scroll and list version;
- ambient input;
- a hover that changes appearance;
- interactions inside a view, which build that view and the views around it while the views beside it are reused (with splice, the views around it are spliced, so only the innermost is built).

The notify-versus-update rules hold at the view level. Verified by the `fast::tests::view_invalidation` group.

A retained record copies and shifts every per-frame output gpux has:

- hitboxes, re-pointed at reused dispatch nodes;
- `hitbox_dispatch_nodes` entries;
- tooltips and deferred draws (attributed to their enclosing record);
- the dispatch subtree, which keeps focus;
- accessed element states and pending element-state transfers;
- line layouts, mouse listeners, input handlers, cursor styles, tab stops and window-control hitboxes;
- `visual_bounds`, which is reported and taken within an element's prepaint and so travels in the DevKit nodes it lands in;
- DevKit nodes, with remapped ids, parents, focus and property setters, truncated on rollback.

Fixed and sticky behaviour is kept for reused views: positioned and top-layer ranges keep their draw-order bands, and a view whose content registers a sticky candidate with a scroll container outside it is laid out by building it, never from its record, so the container pins it from the candidates registered that frame (a candidate's plan is found by the occurrence registered while its layout is requested, which a republished record could not match).

Screen-effect and transition requests owned by `Window` are neither duplicated nor lost.

Verified by the `fast::tests::retained_records` group, with one test per output and a rollback test.

Nothing is drawn from the last frame in any of these cases:

- while refreshing;
- during a drag;
- while the inspector is picking;
- while AccessKit is active;
- for a record whose id is duplicated in a frame;
- with view retention off.

Each case is tested and each reason is counted in `FastStats`. Verified by the `fast::tests::view_invalidation` group.

A view that is dirty only through nesting is copied from the last frame in stretches, with each changed nested view rebuilt in its gap. That requires:

- the view was not notified;
- its own dependencies, offset reads and hovers are unchanged;
- its rem size and text style match;
- every changed nested view is rebuildable on its own and reachable from the view's layout root;
- every deferred draw lies inside a gap.

The rebuilt view inherits the content mask, text style, opacity, transform, sticky and scroll stacks it had in that position. If a gap asks for a different layout, the surrounding view is rebuilt and takes over the prebuilt child rather than building it twice. Dispatch reattachment does not deepen the tree over 1,000 frames.

Verified by the `fast::tests::splice` group.

The core API mirrors Zed PR #62379 and gpui-fast:

- `Window::enable_window_composition() -> Result<WindowComposition>` (idempotent);
- on the controller:
  - `base_surface` and `overlay_surface`;
  - `create_native_surface`, `create_external_gpu_surface` and `create_gpui_surface`;
  - transactional `place_above`, `place_below`, `reparent` and `remove_surface`;
- `Window::with_composition_surface(id, f)`;
- `PlatformSurfaceAttachment` and `PlatformWindow::{enable_window_composition, create_native_surface, set_composition_order, draw_composed}`, with defaults that return an unsupported error or fall back to `draw`.

gpux adds a `native_surface(surface)` element that applies device-pixel bounds during prepaint and registers itself for the scroll-layer bypass. Until composition is enabled, the window draws through today's path with byte-identical scenes. Verified by the `fast::tests::composition` group on the test platform.

Manual native-webview examples exist in `crates/gpui_platform/examples/`:

- WKWebView on macOS;
- WebView2 on Windows;
- WebKitGTK on X11 or XWayland;
- a GPU surface of its own on Wayland.

Each shows a menu, a popover and a dialog with backdrop over live web content. They need dev-dependencies from crates.io only and are not run in CI. Verified by running each on its platform and reviewing the behaviour.

Layer lifecycle follows gpui-fast:

- a layer is promoted after two consecutive scrolled frames;
- each frame decides Composite, Repaint or Bypass;
- **demotion:** content changing on 8 or more of the last 16 frames (4 for `uniform_list`), or a work budget over 32 samples;
- **cooldown:** 60 frames, doubling up to 1,920;
- **eviction:** after 120 idle frames, with a 64 MB tile budget per window;
- every layer is dropped on resize or scale change.

Layers are compiled for every gpux renderer: Linux, Windows, the browser, Android and iOS through wgpu, and macOS through Metal. The tile budget is a per-target constant: 64 MB per window on desktop, 32 MB in the browser and on mobile. The scroll-layers switch also enables device-pixel scroll-offset snapping, glyph-origin quantization and path snapping; with it off, output is today's.

Verified by the `fast::tests::layer_policy` group.

**Scroll-only detection.** A frame is scroll-only when:

- the owner was notified no more times than it was scrolled. Notifications of gpux scroll dependents count as scroll notifications;
- nothing the content read changed;
- no content view was notified;
- no hover changed;
- the owner did not read the offset during render.

**Composite frames** carry, translated by the scroll delta and clipped to the viewport:

- hitboxes;
- listeners;
- the dispatch subtree, which keeps focus;
- cursors, element states, tab stops and window-control hitboxes;
- tooltips, only when the layer did not move.

**Input.** Any input other than the wheel that reaches content which moved since it was painted repaints the layer first. `ScrollHandle::bounds` and `bounds_for_item` are translated when read.

Verified by the `fast::tests::layer_carry` group.

Tiles are 512 device pixels. Each tile is hashed from the primitives that reach it, translated into tile space and including each primitive's transform matrix. The background is baked only when the viewport lies on one opaque solid quad.

Glyphs are culled by reach. Paths are never rasterized into tiles: they are lifted above the tiles, or the layer bypasses.

A layer bypasses when the content holds any of:
- deferred or anchored elements, or `on_children_prepainted`;
- a focused input handler;
- an animation-frame request;
- visual effects or screen-effect regions;
- HTML islands or elements;
- composition `native_surface` elements;
- fixed, sticky or top-layer primitives;
- a translucent or rounded viewport background.

Each bypass is counted with its reason. Verified by the `fast::tests::layer_inputs` group.

The layers oracle plays random histories through a layers-on window and a reference window with layers off and snapping forced on. Histories include:

- wheel scrolls, fractional deltas at scale 1.25 included;
- programmatic scrolls, hovers, clicks, keys, content changes and resizes.

Every frame compares:
- the scenes, with each layer's tile sprites expanded into its content;
- hit tests at 32 random points;
- the positions every listener observed.

Coverage asserts require composited frames, clicks and keys. Verified by the `fast::tests::oracle_layers` group.

The wgpu renderer implements the tile contract:

- tile textures are keyed by layer and generation, in the non-sRGB render-target format, with `RENDER_ATTACHMENT | TEXTURE_BINDING | COPY_SRC`;
- the generation contract holds (a skipped generation invalidates, and dirty tiles wait until shown);
- tiles are rasterized from `LayerFrame::tile_scene` before the main pass, in the same encoder;
- reserved texture ids `0xF000_0000 + key` are resolved in the polychrome sprite path through a premultiplied-input flag. This is an ABI change, made together in Rust and WGSL;
- the gradient dither is seeded by position within the quad;
- the cache is released on resize, scale, alpha mode, device loss and GPU recovery (`force_render`).

Verified in these places:
- the renderer group `cargo test -p gpux-wgpu-renderer --features test-support --lib fast::layers::tests`: assembled tiles, negative tile coordinates, odd translations and a second frame from cache are byte-equal to direct drawing on the software adapter;
- `crates/gpux_conformance/tests/lane_c_scroll_layers.rs`;
- lane B's WebGPU and forced-WebGL2 backend and Dawn specs, for the ABI.

Scroll layers run in the browser on WebGPU and forced WebGL2:

- tile render targets and tile sampling work through the full-record WebGL2 transport;
- the browser tile budget applies;
- HTML islands and elements in scroll content bypass;
- under `diagnostics`, fast-mode counters are published as the browser global `__gpuxFastStats` through `web_globals`.

`tools-node/specs/scroll-layers.spec.ts` scrolls a div and a list in the web host with trusted wheel input on both backends. It asserts composited frames through the global, and screenshot equality with a layers-off page load at DPR 1, plus DPR 2 in the nightly and lane G runs.

Scroll layers run on Android, on Vulkan and on the GL fallback:

- tiles follow the GL ES rules (no early fragment return after sampling, `Rgba8Unorm` atlases on GL, per-buffer uniform binding);
- the mobile tile budget applies;
- tiles are released on `onTrimMemory`, on background, and on surface destruction or recreation, in both host modes;
- the next frame repaints.

The lane E scenes `scroll-layers-*` swipe a div and a list with `adb shell input swipe`. They assert composited frames through the host's counter report and `adb screencap` equality with layers off, using the 16-bit tolerance policy, plus a background-and-resume step that rebuilds the tiles.

Scroll layers run on iOS, on wgpu's Metal backend:

- the mobile tile budget applies;
- tiles are released on `didReceiveMemoryWarning` and on background;
- the next frame repaints.

The lane F scenes `scroll-layers-*` pan a div and a list with Simulator touch input. They assert composited frames through the host's counter report and pixel equality with layers off where the Simulator has a Metal device (skipped with the reason otherwise), plus a background-and-resume step.

These documents are written or updated:

- **`docs/fast-mode.md`** documents:
  - each mechanism, its switches and defaults, and the aliases;
  - the application contract: state outside entities, globals, scroll/list state and ambient input needs `cx.notify()`; notify or write globals only on change; scroll through `ScrollHandle`/`ListState`;
  - the retention verify mode;
  - the composition API, platform matrix and limits;
  - the scroll-layer bypass list;
  - how to run the comparison.
- **`docs/testing.md`** lists the new test groups, the `fast_mode` bench, `gpux-testkit perf fast-mode` and lane G's report.
- **The README** links the page.
- **The gpux skill** gets a fast-mode reference.

Verified by review.
